      "data": {
        "text": "**Defining the External Provider**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on an external provider to source the metric data, which I will need to hard-code into the `aggregate_metric_stats.py` file. The process of getting metrics, which I must identify in code, has begun.\n\n\n**Implementing the Retrieval Logic**\n\nI'm now structuring the `aggregate_metric_stats.py` file. I'm importing the `google.cloud.monitoring_v3` and `google.api_core.datetime_helpers` libraries. My focus is the `get_metric_stats` function, which will receive metric parameters and construct a `ListTimeSeriesRequest` with details for the Google Cloud Monitoring API. The data will then be processed, to ultimately aggregate statistics. I'm focusing on crafting the correct query.\n\n\n**Establishing the Python File**\n\nI'm now writing the Python code for `aggregate_metric_stats.py`, clearly identifying `google.cloud.monitoring_v3` as the provider. I've structured a function `get_aggregated_metric_stats` to handle querying Google Cloud Monitoring. It receives inputs like project ID, metric type, and time window. The code constructs the `ListTimeSeriesRequest`, calls `list_time_series`, and processes the results. I'm focusing on initializing the `MetricServiceClient`, `TimeInterval`, and `Aggregation` objects.\n\n\n",
        "time": {
          "end": 1789861648283,
          "start": 1789861642912
        },
        "type": "reasoning"
      },
      "id": "prt_0bc1142a0001wYnu9k9k2cPGdU",
      "message_id": "msg_0bc113e5a001b28Rpo80y6m0xg",
